    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d466">

                <pb facs="0798" n="780" sameAs="frus:frus1951v06p1/pg780"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Department of Defense Files: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Commander in Chief of United States Forces in the
                        Far East</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">MacArthur</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Joint Chiefs of Staff </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-01-04">January 4,
                        1951</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>C 52713. Reference DA 80222.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d466-f1"/> There is no question
                    as to the vulnerability of Hokkaido to Soviet amphibious and/or airborne attack
                    nor as to the availability of USSR forces for such operations. The Department is
                    in better position to evaluate Soviet intentions with respect to Hokkaido from
                    studies of global intelligence than is this theater. So far as our intelligence
                    reports, there is no local indication of Soviet special preparation to attack
                    Hokkaido. Such an attack would undoubtedly precipitate a global war. It does not
                    appear possible that the presence of Mr. Dulles in Japan could have the
                    slightest effect upon Soviet decisions to initiate such a war.</p>
                <pb facs="0799" n="781" xml:id="pg781"/>
                <p>The situation in Korea at present obviously would not permit the withdrawal of
                    any elements of our forces there without endangering that part of the command
                    which remained under present commitment. My concern for the security of Japan
                    resulted in my considered recommendation as stated in C–51559<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d466-f2"/> that the four national guard divisions now on active duty
                    should be promptly deployed to Japan for completion of their projected training
                    and to provide emergency protection.</p>
                <p>The desirability of a Japanese peace treaty from both a military and political
                    stand point is of such urgency that all practical measures should be taken
                    without delay to negotiate such an agreement. However, it might be prudent to
                    accept a delay in Mr. Dulles’ arrival if reinforcements are to be moved from the
                    ZI to Japan in six weeks, the possibility of which is suggested in your
                    reference message.</p>
            </div>
